result:
The leak is at `loop.C:4`. Each iteration does `new TH1F(...)` and the pointer is never deleted. That gives 10^6 histograms by the end of the loop.

ROOT makes this worse than a plain `new` leak. A `TH1` is added to `gDirectory`, which is `gROOT` here, when it is constructed. So the directory's object list keeps a reference to every histogram, and each one also stays registered under its name. Even if you dropped the pointer, the memory would not be released. Each histogram also costs a `Form` string and a name lookup, so the list gets slower as it grows.

Which fix is right depends on whether you need the histograms to outlive the loop.

**1. You only need a scratch histogram per event.** Create it once and reuse it:
```cpp
void loop() {
  TH1F h("h", "", 10, 0, 1);
  for (int i = 0; i < 1000000; ++i) {
    h.Reset();
    h.Fill(0.5);
    // use h
  }
}
```

**2. You need a fresh object each iteration.** Delete it, which also unregisters it from the directory:
```cpp
for (int i = 0; i < 1000000; ++i) {
  auto h = std::make_unique<TH1F>(Form("h%d", i), "", 10, 0, 1);
  h->Fill(0.5);
}
```
If the histograms are only temporary, you can also call `TH1::AddDirectory(kFALSE)` first. Then they never register with the directory, and `delete` or a smart pointer is enough.

**3. You really need all 10^6 histograms.** That will use a lot of memory however you write it. Write each one to a `TFile` and delete it as you go, or use a single `TH2` or `TTree` instead.

I only had `loop.C` to go on. If your real macro looks different, for example it also has a `TTree`, `TCanvas` or `TFile` inside the loop, send it and I'll check it for the same pattern. I haven't run this, so I haven't measured the memory use before or after the fix.

